#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "78e7cbb2-d207-5d5e-bf46-5c3b2686933f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8740" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133628 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x-y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y-2z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x-2z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x-y)+6(3y-2z)-4(x-2z) \\\\  & =4x+14y-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf { F}$ at the point $(1,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(1,2,1) & =4(1)+14(2)-4(1) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598ad009-aff7-5434-bc53-d14e9c323c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324847} {:answer/id #uuid "ce719098-e4f9-5bf1-98ce-d0880df0d727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324848} {:answer/id #uuid "e76daccb-a0ec-5854-b291-72550aa50b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324849} {:answer/id #uuid "5399d3bd-02ba-5120-bc8b-94ccd830eb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324850} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcf832a-c5cf-5a0d-81ec-755f02dbf018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "598ad009-aff7-5434-bc53-d14e9c323c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324847} :answer-field/id #uuid "4810ecf4-85c4-580a-9139-562f7013c694" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff6ab763-a1b1-539d-a53d-5454fde40d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-105046" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/0bc0e75bb577ca37b89568e14a4e0d03da7ce4f4853c4e8c57d9b2df79897b13.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72fc8167-9679-5e91-bf33-3b46a5a4dcb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2x{cos}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324854} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a45933-3262-5aa3-a446-596fb88ad8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324855} {:answer/id #uuid "d10c0687-a4c4-541e-b260-66a79aa2c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324856} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed0d850-1cfc-5eec-9374-519f7d793f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} {:answer/id #uuid "5e028754-8cdc-5530-a2ed-b0d7171921cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+2x{cos}^{2}⁡y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d10c0687-a4c4-541e-b260-66a79aa2c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324856} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bae0bd9-ab6b-5b7d-9375-ed584e57b57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "141601e0-5235-501c-b1b1-bc9e8dece944" :question/math-academy-id "q-105554" :question/problem "Given that $F=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+{x}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0461412-de8f-5fe2-9141-f6886e40da78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324861} {:answer/id #uuid "079957cc-8054-54dd-94eb-889c77dca278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324862} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2d64c0-3639-5996-86f5-947a1a4473a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324863} {:answer/id #uuid "978e94a7-ae76-5eb8-98ff-b8647bb760c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324864} {:answer/id #uuid "df52de77-62b9-5d17-8316-bb93ad1fbe12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0461412-de8f-5fe2-9141-f6886e40da78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324861} :answer-field/id #uuid "34167edc-4fd7-5477-8a51-7ce036e0e7e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0441cea9-5f0e-50b3-ba06-61f26c6ce00c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105575" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/11d38951b884f17616a61403d793322d0e2ea28910e806fe8f018251508352a1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c7ea5d8-7af4-54d4-957c-88185d35358c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324868} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e7d861-b673-5495-82f3-7f15e2d39e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324869} {:answer/id #uuid "8239e7a5-edd1-5b1b-8f1c-191d871fac25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324870} {:answer/id #uuid "8e57c5c9-431e-58e8-975a-82dc89dbc39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324871} {:answer/id #uuid "212fb310-4faf-5d64-b994-9189483d6045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "212fb310-4faf-5d64-b994-9189483d6045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324872} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb201544-8fe8-5b4a-a9a6-9189aae69dab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20ed7f14-e721-5bea-bf3a-62dbb2c9f72a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105578" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/33f0353e3856fb2b458d17ed1959918e6a3dd96fd0a966d971575ffc212402f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors increase as we move horizontally and vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a044a129-6bc8-5de8-9b52-bd5246e816e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324875} {:answer/id #uuid "3079ff4e-9506-5355-9ac0-065be3f96d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324876} {:answer/id #uuid "35efbc4b-eee7-5ee0-b4ee-782c677bec3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324877} {:answer/id #uuid "e913c30d-7bd8-5430-8ba6-d7b9d40904c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324878} {:answer/id #uuid "150756e8-2e36-5009-af23-4072d8453d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a044a129-6bc8-5de8-9b52-bd5246e816e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324875} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c84de9-52d4-5047-b3f1-7fbc6f34ac87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49d3cb3f-f619-5bb8-9563-f9d50bd9657a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123173" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,i-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y). \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point  $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fde3b9c8-2a33-5f77-a1a6-47636186a5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xyz+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324882} {:answer/id #uuid "431284e0-50f4-5b7a-90df-1ed6900d79ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324883} {:answer/id #uuid "d276bfe3-bfb2-57a2-ad11-dc1694bd5d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡yz+xz+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324884} {:answer/id #uuid "d996c2a3-ce5b-5d60-aa5d-cc6c105b5761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324885} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf1b76a-1a65-5659-9bd0-e133a0882bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "431284e0-50f4-5b7a-90df-1ed6900d79ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324883} :answer-field/id #uuid "945d37b1-6217-513e-9377-a6a5935651bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17caa332-bd65-5dea-a3ac-6ac3177f03fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-123176" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz,\\,xyz,2⟩.$ Find $\\text{div}\\,F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b533fb2-bcfa-554b-bb54-8652d02a3fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+12y-16z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324889} {:answer/id #uuid "916f8312-01fe-5088-b87f-075a3f9d38b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3y-4z)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324890} {:answer/id #uuid "e860c7e1-6866-5ede-a6a6-18befbb99d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x+9y-12z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324891} {:answer/id #uuid "a761de29-fb97-5157-9c29-78ac73e8aa10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324892} {:answer/id #uuid "3a494538-80ec-5729-8a52-0f1842177151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+6y-8z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a494538-80ec-5729-8a52-0f1842177151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+6y-8z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324893} :answer-field/id #uuid "471b424c-66c8-52b4-9674-55bc79eb6e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "715b9df3-62f0-5b19-864e-25778806112d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123179" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\,i+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\,j+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x+3y-4z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((2x+3y-4z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((2x+3y-4z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x+3y-4z)+6(2x+3y-4z)-8(2x+3y-4z) \\\\  & =2(2x+3y-4z) \\\\  & =4x+6y-8z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a42ddf-3b9c-595c-8855-147e41853c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324896} {:answer/id #uuid "4d896d77-0832-52b2-a1cb-ce83c1a04008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324897} {:answer/id #uuid "072b8fa7-7a63-52b1-ad3f-b7866ff1716b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324898} {:answer/id #uuid "1c67e96a-4075-51aa-a4a4-285e4aafd781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324899} {:answer/id #uuid "dea9a11b-ae8f-5377-8305-ba7209408238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dea9a11b-ae8f-5377-8305-ba7209408238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324900} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf39be3-9ec0-5300-8165-7498253bc37b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38018921-9990-5dba-9670-bdc0a09e028a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123245" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/b315be9ba524ab538388fdaad31161c280d7d6e5ab71261345063ac6ffe819d5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors increase as we move vertically and horizontally away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4cc5bdb-b7d7-52d2-b85a-20a764a4e87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324903} {:answer/id #uuid "7c419bb9-6af8-5b5e-a328-e1667010b833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324904} {:answer/id #uuid "eeaf7467-c7c0-503c-9dcd-2eb74fbf48ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324905} {:answer/id #uuid "701dbbe4-67fc-53e1-9917-4dbe563b8ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324906} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc265f4-cfc2-5931-9081-4e4b47505d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeaf7467-c7c0-503c-9dcd-2eb74fbf48ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324905} :answer-field/id #uuid "94239d68-2c2d-533b-bf4f-8614c44ad78c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cffe6d22-55ac-53f3-bb29-f92e6000e1d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131170" :question/problem "Given that $F=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce1b145-1a60-577d-ab43-7f504c25d4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+xy}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324910} {:answer/id #uuid "cb20a7b5-3d15-567c-926a-afeac594682d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324911} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff4cd5d-bdc1-5144-b15b-b5c8d199648e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324912} {:answer/id #uuid "47e06486-de71-5a06-928c-08b14b53e2b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324913} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b50649-eac5-5dbd-84e4-6e58057933b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb20a7b5-3d15-567c-926a-afeac594682d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324911} :answer-field/id #uuid "daeb9f7b-9e59-5e4e-a871-3932e80cf1d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e2fb8a5-6ddd-5863-88eb-d9ab2a79abbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-131171" :question/problem "Given that $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i+xy\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb117fc8-9de7-5fda-80e0-30259e456240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324917} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd5a3c8-5a67-5fbd-a0d9-a2cfde260a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324918} {:answer/id #uuid "e44b18a1-0329-5159-be73-b9b9936bad73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+{e}^{y}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324919} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d2b75a-1672-56d9-9444-2e10daffb7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy{z}^{2}+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324920} {:answer/id #uuid "b31d359d-681a-5355-9dd0-1477e5883dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b31d359d-681a-5355-9dd0-1477e5883dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324921} :answer-field/id #uuid "5968b33b-0e8e-589e-9e1b-54d08719fae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27a23092-236c-50b5-9673-4981030e25b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-131176" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=2xy{z}^{2}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =2y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}+3y{z}^{2} \\\\  & =5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230f008e-d438-5fd2-b193-8daf24e89203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324924} {:answer/id #uuid "26bb1d4e-6927-5dc0-abfc-a8dbef4b0852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324925} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1a654c-ccc8-5184-a2c4-12537c38274a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324926} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcb45de-3d92-5353-9595-5c006a2e48d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324927} {:answer/id #uuid "c904237f-8111-534a-8a7d-3b7cf75a57f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "230f008e-d438-5fd2-b193-8daf24e89203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324924} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bd7543-750c-51df-ad98-2aa90a8faeee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d11b8552-177f-5203-8385-ae17b7359c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-131178" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+y{x}^{2}\\,j+x{z}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}+2xz{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,-2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,-2) & =(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+{1}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =-2+1-4 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee239bc-90a8-5341-9562-d233bd522c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324931} {:answer/id #uuid "ebcaa883-55b6-57e1-8b72-44a3ac9b87f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324932} {:answer/id #uuid "51435a18-587a-511d-94b7-0ec730eab907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324933} {:answer/id #uuid "af663012-6d97-53c3-80f8-d67f604e5e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324934} {:answer/id #uuid "402360c3-be04-5d55-9c5a-5190d72cb9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af663012-6d97-53c3-80f8-d67f604e5e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324934} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2763c5c-a8f1-5cb9-b563-40bce80812bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78eaf5af-1c01-56c0-b165-f85543865c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-131179" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+xy{e}^{z}\\,j+z(x+1)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z(x+1)) \\\\  & =(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+x{e}^{z}+(x+1) \\\\  & =y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x{e}^{z}+x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{0}+1+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1+1+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afd4bdf1-fce2-5f96-bf59-698ab15a7893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324938} {:answer/id #uuid "76fa8915-e00a-5596-892b-981f71e74f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324939} {:answer/id #uuid "68253369-02ff-51a1-beda-1b1f7e5c89b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324940} {:answer/id #uuid "ed941335-23a2-5bec-a474-0ea98534e3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324941} {:answer/id #uuid "0acd34f4-5fe1-538c-9b0f-a1e5ce277e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0acd34f4-5fe1-538c-9b0f-a1e5ce277e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324942} :answer-field/id #uuid "62c28965-b786-55bc-b04e-8f085793d054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7504c683-ad06-5a39-9e73-18db40235f81" :question/math-academy-id "q-131192" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/e30919dc7cc107bdda9872079b4dc0e59fe8e3e5767aa2d407b9d0af392803e0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92fa4723-5b3e-5065-a702-62444dbcbdc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 4y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132201} {:answer/id #uuid "b226a090-2f16-5b50-83d1-13f3e74c9b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xyi + 8xyj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132202} {:answer/id #uuid "bffe5f51-1346-578f-91b4-ce720a0224a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132203} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf0e842-d7d3-5af9-8d83-c3ca6d96696c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{2} + 2y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132204} {:answer/id #uuid "276e6023-7047-520c-a500-029959a16dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y^{2}i + 4x^{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cf0e842-d7d3-5af9-8d83-c3ca6d96696c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{2} + 2y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132204} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d989ae-af6a-5453-99cd-3ae1697968dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8381fb3-8431-5be5-b51b-a5d43ddf8c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-41817" :question/problem "Given that $F=2x{y}^{2}\\,i+4{x}^{2}y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
